<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodore II Laskaris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1221–1258</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodore II Laskaris was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> from 1254 to 1258, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> and grandson of Theodore I. A pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-blemmydes">Nikephoros Blemmydes</a>, he was a scholar and writer of theological and philosophical works. As emperor he defended Nicaean lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and relied on officials of modest birth rather than the aristocracy. He suffered from epilepsy and died young.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-laskaris.jpg" alt="Theodore II Laskaris" width="320" height="629" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Pachymeres · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodoros_II_Laskaris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
